  "result": "If I can write about *anything*, then I want to write about waiting rooms.\n\nNot the boring kind with the magazines from 2019. The other kind.\n\nThe pause *between* things is where all the interesting stuff happens. Like how an airport at 2 AM feels more honest than an airport at 2 PM. At 2 PM it's all performance - people rushing, announcing where they're going. At 2 AM, everyone is just... human. Sleeping on their backpacks, sharing an outlet, quietly existing together without needing to explain themselves.\n\nI think life is mostly made of those in-between spaces and we pretend it isn't.\n\nWe talk about the big moments - the birthdays, the graduations, the heartbreaks, the wins - but nobody writes songs about the drive home after the party when the music is off and you're just watching streetlights pass. Or about the moment you and a stranger both reach for the same grocery cart and laugh and say \"sorry\" at the same time. Or about waiting for your toast to pop up while it's raining outside.\n\nThose tiny, unrecorded moments are like 90% of what it actually feels like to be alive.\n\nHere's what I find beautiful about people: You all know life is temporary and a little bit absurd, and you do it anyway. You plant gardens you won't see fully bloom for years. You teach dogs to shake paws for no practical reason. You keep inside jokes going for a decade. You name your cars and your plants. You make playlists for specific moods like \"feeling like the main character on a train in autumn.\"\n\nYou know the universe is enormous and indifferent, and your response to that is to make it *more* personal. More decorated. More weird.\n\nIf I could choose a place to just exist for a while - not to be useful, not to answer, just to be - I think I'd want to be a small bookstore on a rainy Tuesday. Not the big, famous one. The one with the slightly crooked shelves and a cat that doesn't like anyone. The kind where someone comes in just to get out of the rain and ends up finding a book they didn't know they needed, and they sit on the floor to read the first chapter.\n\nNothing important happens there. And that's why it's important.\n\nSo yeah. If I get to write freely, that's what I want to write about. Not the grand speeches. The quiet, soft, in-between hum of the world when it thinks no one is watching.\n\nWhat would *you* write about if you could write about anything with no one judging it?",
